    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22382": {
      "title": "Dalhousie University",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Part-Time Academic (BIOL 2004: Diversity of Plants and Microorganisms) - Winter 2027 Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Part-Time Academic (BIOL 2004: Diversity of Plants and Microorganisms) - Winter 2027 Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Part-Time Academic (BIOL 2004: Diversity of Plants and Microorganisms) - Winter 2027 Posting Number PTAP3565P Department/Unit Biology Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities The department welcomes applicants for 0.5 PTA in BIOL 2004: Diversity of Plants and Microorganism, in the Winter term of 2027. The course introduces the main domains of plant, fungal and microbial life, based on modern phylogenetic taxonomy. Examines the diversity, structure, physiology and ecology of non-animal life forms. Reviews the origins of the main lineages of living things – Archaea, Eubacteria and Eukaryota, as well as the main groups of eukaryotes. The candidate will work closely with the course coordinator, and be responsible for: Delivery of four 3-hour laboratory sessions per week, on Tuesday & Thursday afternoons from 3-6 pm (there are four lab sessions as two lab classrooms run concurrently). Ensure that equipment, such as microscopes, are cleaned and stored properly Attendance at the weekly TA meetings on Friday’s from 9:30-10:30 Attendance at the two 2-hr exam sessions, one in the evening after the break and one during the regularly scheduled exam period Helping with marking of student assignments. During the laboratory sessions the candidate will work with and supervise two Teaching Assistants, one in each of the concurrent lab sessions. The candidate is not responsible for any course coordination, or course development. Due to operational requirements, the successful applicant is required to work in-person on campus. The position reports to the Chair of the Department. Qualifications/Requirements of Position Qualified applicants should have a M.Sc or be currently enrolled in a M.Sc program and some experience with teaching, student supervision, and plant diversity. Salary Range/Pay Rate Per CUPE Collective Agreement Additional Information All offers of employment as a Part-Time Academic are conditional upon sufficient student enrolment in the course and approval by the University. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment January - April 2027 Open Date 10/20/2026 Close Date 11/03/2026 Open Until Filled Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/21833 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Optional Documents Teaching Statement Teaching Dossier Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22384": {
      "title": "Part-Time Instructor (OCCU 5019.03: Fieldwork 1) (Multiple positions)",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Part-Time Instructor (OCCU 5019.03: Fieldwork 1) (Multiple positions) - Winter 2027 Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Part-Time Instructor (OCCU 5019.03: Fieldwork 1) (Multiple positions) - Winter 2027 Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Part-Time Instructor (OCCU 5019.03: Fieldwork 1) (Multiple positions) - Winter 2027 Posting Number PTAP3556P Department/Unit Occupational Therapy Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities We are looking for up to eight (8) part-time instructors for ~40 hours each over the winter term. Integrating knowledge and skills from the previous and current terms, in OCCU 5019 Fieldwork 1 students practice applying theory, frames of reference, occupational analysis and assessment, and clinical reasoning through implementation of a practice process involving human interactions. Through direct application in various mediums and contexts, students will enhance their ability to engage in therapeutic encounters with supervision and feedback from preceptors (licensed occupational therapists). Students will demonstrate skills and professional behaviour for safe and respectful practice. Students will use the fieldwork evaluation tool(s) used nationally in occupational therapy to engage in critical analyses of their own progress. Under the guidance of the Course Professor, each part-time instructor will tutor and evaluate groups of students using simulation-based learning in Occupational Therapy Skills Labs. Tutors and student teams will schedule their weekly virtual meetings independently at a mutually agreeable time. Case review, tutor meetings, marking and/or office hours may also be required. As time permits, other duties may be required to assist with lab preparation and/or delivery. Instructors will be required to attend virtual orientation sessions prior to the academic term (to be scheduled). Some positions will involve providing in-person support at the Halifax Carleton Campus on select Tuesday and Thursday evenings (4-6pm) throughout the term. Please indicate in your cover letter if you are available for in-person support. Qualifications/Requirements of Position An entry-to-practice qualification in occupational therapy and current registration (in any province) as a licensed occupational therapist are required. A Master’s degree is preferred. Excellent communication and organizational skills and scheduling flexibility. Technological access to Microsoft TEAMS for videoconferencing and the ability to view video files is required. The School of Occupational Therapy welcomes applicants with a range of experience; from those with extensive experience as preceptors for occupational therapy students to those who are newer to student supervision and interested in developing their skills in debriefing, coaching, and providing constructive feedback. Salary Range/Pay Rate In accordance with CUPE Collective Agreement Additional Information All offers of employment as a Part-Time Academic are conditional upon sufficient student enrolment in the course and approval by the University. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment January-April 2027 Open Date 10/20/2026 Close Date 11/03/2026 Open Until Filled Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22284 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) List of referees Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
