    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22402": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Postdoctoral Fellow - Social and Structural Determinants of Youth Health in NS Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Postdoctoral Fellow - Social and Structural Determinants of Youth Health in NS Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Social and Structural Determinants of Youth Health in NS Posting Number PTAP3571P Department/Unit Social Work Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities About the Organization : The Resilience Research Centre ( RRC ), based at Dalhousie University, collaborates with local, national and international institutions to conduct innovative research exploring pathways to resilience across cultures. That work focuses on explaining how children, youth and adults thrive in family, school, workplace and community settings under stress. The RRC is a world-renowned resource hub of resilience expertise and tools to support individuals and communities on their path to psychological, social, cultural and physical well-being. The RRC is an international group of scholars, practitioners and policymakers from more than 20 countries who collaborate on research and knowledge mobilization projects. This is an excellent opportunity for the right candidate to build a strong network of contacts and increase their publications. Job Summary : The Resilience Research Centre invites applications for a one-year, full-time Postdoctoral Fellow ( PDF ) position. Under the supervision of Dr. Jan Holtge, the PDF will work on a mixed-methods research project funded by Research Nova Scotia to identify the unique characteristics of communities excelling in comprehensive youth (12-19 years) health resilience. This is the second phase of a sequential research project in which the team analyzed administrative health data to identify communities with exceptionally high and low levels of healthcare use and recorded diagnoses among youth across Nova Scotia. Key responsibilities : The successful candidate will participate in all activities related to the project, including: Build and maintain relationships with local and provincial stakeholders. Co-research with local youth advisors. Recruitment of participants. Carry out a quantitative survey study and participatory mapping with youth. Analyze empirical mixed-methods research data. Organize meetings, workshops, and community outreach. Prepare academic publications and research reports. Supervise and train research assistants in project-relevant methods. Communicate to the project team developments/progress and results of research activities, ensuring that relevant information and issues in the implementation of project/research activities are captured in as comprehensive and timely a manner as possible. Qualifications/Requirements of Position The successful PDF candidate must possess: Completed or all but dissertation ( ABD ) a PhD in the last 5 years in a related field such as social work, sociology, or psychology. Demonstrated experience with recruiting youth. Demonstrated experience with empirical social research, including quantitative and/or qualitative data collection methods. Demonstrated experience with quantitative and/or qualitative data analysis methods. Proven track record of research and publications relevant to the position. Proficiency in English with strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational, time and project management skills. Salary Range/Pay Rate $62,000 Additional Information Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment 1 year, anticipated start date of January 11, 2027 Open Date 10/22/2026 Close Date 11/06/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22344 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Sample Publication(s) List of referees Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
