    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22445": {
      "title": "Dalhousie University",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Part-Time Academic (ARCH 5311: Professional Practice) - Winter 2027 Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Part-Time Academic (ARCH 5311: Professional Practice) - Winter 2027 Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Part-Time Academic (ARCH 5311: Professional Practice) - Winter 2027 Posting Number PTAP3586P Department/Unit Architecture Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities The School of Architecture at Dalhousie University invites applications for a Part-Time Academic Instructor position in the Master of Architecture (MArch) Program. The Instructor will teach a 3-credit graduate course examining the professional, ethical, legal, and organizational dimensions of architectural practice. The course addresses professional responsibility, regulatory frameworks, business structures, and the management of architectural practice, including contracts, project delivery, financial management, costing techniques, and contract administration. Students develop an understanding of the responsibilities of architects within the profession, the construction industry, and society. The Instructor will be responsible for developing and delivering course content through lectures, seminars, case studies, and discussions of contemporary professional practice. Prior course outlines and structures are available. The Instructor is invited to bring their professional experience and disciplinary perspective to the course structure, content, and delivery. Responsibilities include course planning, instruction, student assessment, and coordination of guest lectures or contributions from practicing professionals, where appropriate. Course Description, ARCH 5311 Professional Practice: This course studies principles of professional ethics, partnerships, corporate practices, professional responsibility, and legal aspects of architectural practice. It also considers issues in practice management (contracts, reference documents, finance, costing techniques, and contract administration). CREDIT HOURS : 3 FORMAT : Lecture Seminar Qualifications/Requirements of Position Education and Experience M.Arch or equivalent Master’s degree in architecture or a related field. Professional experience in architectural practice, preferably within a Canadian context. Prior university-level teaching experience or comparable experience in professional education or mentorship. Record of professional, scholarly, or other relevant accomplishments. Professional registration as an architect in Canada is considered an asset. Professional Competencies Ability to communicate complex ideas effectively and facilitate student learning. Ability to work cooperatively with diverse faculty and student populations. Demonstrated knowledge of architectural professional practice, including ethics, professional responsibility, regulatory frameworks, and practice management. Demonstrated engagement with one or more of the following areas is considered an asset: Architectural contracts, procurement, and project delivery methods. Practice management, business structures, financial management, and project costing. Construction administration, professional liability, and risk management. Emerging models of architectural practice, including interdisciplinary collaboration and evolving professional responsibilities. Equity, sustainability, and social responsibility in professional practice. Salary Range/Pay Rate Per CUPE Collective Agreement Additional Information All offers of employment as a Part-Time Academic are conditional upon sufficient student enrolment in the course and approval by the University. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment January-April 2027 Open Date 10/27/2026 Close Date 11/10/2026 Open Until Filled Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22411 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Teaching Dossier Portfolio List of referees Optional Documents Teaching Statement Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
