    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22416": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Postdoctoral Fellow - Child and Youth Well-being in Nova Scotia Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Postdoctoral Fellow - Child and Youth Well-being in Nova Scotia Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Postdoctoral Fellow - Child and Youth Well-being in Nova Scotia Posting Number PTAP3562P Department/Unit Social Work Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities The successful candidate will be cross-appointed at the Centre for Planetary Health and Sustainable Care and the Resilience Research Centre at Dalhousie University. The Centre for Planetary Health and Sustainable Care is an interdisciplinary research and training environment committed to exploring the complex interconnections between human health, environmental sustainability, and equitable care systems. The centre conducts research and education that addresses critical global challenges, fostering solutions that promote the wellbeing of both people and the planet. The Resilience Research Centre collaborates with local, national and international institutions to conduct innovative research exploring pathways to resilience across cultures. That work focuses on explaining how children, youth and adults thrive in family, school, workplace and community settings under stress. The Centre is a world-renowned resource hub of resilience expertise and tools to support individuals and communities on their path to psychological, social, cultural and physical well-being. The Centre for Planetary Health and Sustainable Care and the Resilience Research Centre invite applications for a Postdoctoral Fellow ( PDF ) for a two-year position (with possibility for renewal). The PDF will work on a project funded by the Government of Nova Scotia’s Department of Health and Wellness to bring together provincial administrative records, provincial and national surveys, and academic research to create the most comprehensive picture of child and youth well-being in Nova Scotia to date and lay the groundwork for a permanent, coordinated longitudinal monitoring system that tracks development from childhood into young adulthood in Nova Scotia. Due to data-access requirements, the PDF (Postdoctoral Fellow) must reside in Nova Scotia. The Postdoctoral Fellow will work with a larger provincial and multidisciplinary team led by Dr. Jan Höltge and Dr. Daniel Rainham and will be based at Dalhousie University in Halifax, Nova Scotia. Key responsibilities : The successful candidate will participate in all activities related to the project, including: Networking with decision-makers and data holders across sectors such as Nova Scotia Health, the Department of Health and Wellness, and the Department of Education & Early Childhood Development. Statistical and geospatial analysis of multisectoral data, including data management and linkage. Organizing meetings, workshops, and community outreach. Preparing academic publications and research reports. Supervise and train research assistants in project-relevant methods. Communicate to project team developments/progress and results of research activities ensuring that relevant information and issues in the implementation of project/research activities are captured in as comprehensive and timely manner as possible. Qualifications/Requirements of Position The ideal candidate will have advanced training in epidemiology, health geography, or a related discipline, and provides evidence of applying spatial analysis in health research. Preferred candidates will have research expertise in epidemiology or health geography, biostatistics, experience in database development, be a strong communicator (writer), show evidence of collaboration with governmental institutions, and be an organized project manager. The successful PDF candidate must possess: Completed or all but dissertation ( ABD ) a PhD (or equivalent) in the last 5 years in a related field such as epidemiology and health geography. Demonstrated experience with quantitative methods (biostatistics and geospatial analyses), and ideally analysis using R and spatial analysis software (ArcPro, QGIS ). Proven track record of research and publications relevant to the position. Proficiency in English with strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational, time and project management skills Salary Range/Pay Rate $66,701.72 Additional Information Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment 2 years, anticipated start date of December 1, 2026 Open Date 10/14/2026 Close Date 11/10/2026 Open Until Filled No Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22325 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Sample Publication(s) List of referees Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22447": {
      "title": "Dalhousie University",
      "description": "Dalhousie University | Part-Time Academic (ARCH 4502: Representation) - Winter 2027 Skip to Main Content JOBS AT DALHOUSIE UNIVERSITY Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help About Dalhousie The Dalhousie Impact Working at Dal Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Talent Pathways Student Employment Hiring a Student FAQS for applicants Part-Time Academic (ARCH 4502: Representation) - Winter 2027 Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Position Title Part-Time Academic (ARCH 4502: Representation) - Winter 2027 Posting Number PTAP3588P Department/Unit Architecture Location Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Job Summary & Key Responsibilities The School of Architecture at Dalhousie University invites applications for a Part-Time Academic Instructor position in the Bachelor of Environmental Design Studies ( BEDS ) Architecture Program. BEDS is a pre-professional undergraduate Architecture degree. Students enroll in the BEDS degree in their third year of undergraduate education, entering a two-year sequence. The Instructor will teach a 3-credit undergraduate course exploring the use of digital coding, computational modelling, and fabrication in architectural design processes. The course introduces students to the use of datasets, parametric modelling, and algorithmic design methods to generate and evaluate complex geometric forms. Students develop techniques for translating computational design processes into architectural drawings, digital renderings, and physical prototypes, and evaluate design outcomes in relation to project intentions, constraints, and opportunities. The Instructor will be responsible for developing and delivering course content through lectures, demonstrations, and studio-based instruction. Prior course outlines and structures are available. The Instructor is invited to bring their disciplinary perspective and expertise to the course structure, content, and delivery. Responsibilities include course planning, instruction in digital design tools and workflows, development of assignments, student assessment, and coordination of digital fabrication activities where applicable. The course should emphasize the relationship between computational methods, design intentions, and material realization. Course Description, ARCH 4502 Representation: This course studies the use of digital coding, modeling, and fabrication in processes of form-making for design. Datasets and parametric modeling are used to generate complex geometric forms; represented as technical drawings, renderings, and physical prototypes; then evaluated in relation to initial project intentions, constraints, and opportunities. CREDIT HOURS : 3 FORMAT : Lecture Studio Qualifications/Requirements of Position Education and Experience M.Arch or equivalent Master’s degree in architecture, computational design, or a related field. Prior university-level teaching experience or comparable experience in computational design education. Record of scholarly, creative, professional, or other relevant accomplishments involving computational design and digital fabrication. Professional Competencies Ability to communicate complex technical and design concepts effectively and facilitate student learning. Ability to work cooperatively with diverse faculty and student populations. Demonstrated proficiency in parametric modelling, computational design, and digital representation workflows. Demonstrated engagement with one or more of the following areas is considered an asset: Algorithmic design, visual programming, and scripting for architectural applications. Data-driven design, geometric analysis, and computational form generation. Digital fabrication, including CNC machining, robotic fabrication, and additive manufacturing. Integration of computational design with material systems, fabrication constraints, and physical prototyping. Computational simulation, performance-based design, and environmental analysis. Advanced digital visualization, modelling, and representation techniques. In addition to a cover letter and current CV, candidates should include in their application: a Portfolio of work demonstrating their individual academic, creative and/or professional accomplishments a Teaching Dossier teaching accomplishments demonstrated through work by students the Portfolio and Teaching Dossier may be combined as a single PDF Salary Range/Pay Rate Per CUPE Collective Agreement Additional Information All offers of employment as a Part-Time Academic are conditional upon sufficient student enrolment in the course and approval by the University. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Dalhousie University commits to achieving inclusive excellence through continually championing equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility. The university encourages applications from Indigenous Peoples of Turtle Island (especially Mi’kmaq), persons of Black/African descent (especially African Nova Scotians), and members of other racialized groups, persons with disabilities, women, persons identifying as members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities, and all candidates who would contribute to the diversity of our community. In accordance with our Employment Equity Policy, preference will be given in hiring processes to candidates who self-identify as members of one or more of the equity-deserving groups listed above. For more information, including details related to our Employment Equity Policy and Plan and definitions of equity-deserving groups please review our Employment Equity information. If you require any support for the purpose of accommodation, such as technical aids or alternative arrangements, please let us know of these needs and how we can be of assistance. Dalhousie University is committed to ensuring all candidates have full, fair, and equitable participation in the hiring process. Our complete Accommodation Policy can be viewed here . Posting Detail Information Term of appointment January-April 2027 Open Date 10/27/2026 Close Date 11/10/2026 Open Until Filled Quick Link for Direct Access to Posting https://dal.peopleadmin.ca/postings/22413 Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Résumé / Curriculum Vitae (CV) Teaching Dossier Portfolio List of referees Optional Documents Teaching Statement Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Dalhousie University Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada  B3H 4R2 1-902-494-2211 Agricultural Campus Truro, Nova Scotia, Canada  B2N 5E3 1-902-893-6600 Contact Us | Campus Directory | Campus Maps Media Centre | Privacy Statement Terms of Use To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
